1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,778
Research Computing on Explorer and AICR. AICR Introduction. What Northeastern's AI

2
00:00:03,798 --> 00:00:10,338
cluster is, how to log in, and how to run your first job, so you can use its GPUs

3
00:00:10,358 --> 00:00:12,410
instead of waiting on your laptop.

4
00:00:12,480 --> 00:00:16,701
Hallo. This is Liam, in for Bear. First, a correction. AICR is not the cluster where

5
00:00:16,721 --> 00:00:21,473
you build and test your AI work. It's the cluster where you run the big jobs. It's a

6
00:00:21,493 --> 00:00:25,184
shared GPU cluster for AI research in Massachusetts, and this lecture covers what it

7
00:00:25,204 --> 00:00:29,160
is, how you get on, where your files live, and how you run a job.

8
00:00:29,230 --> 00:00:33,374
Six terms. An HPC cluster: many computers wired together and shared by many people.

9
00:00:33,394 --> 00:00:38,132
A GPU: a chip that does AI math far faster than a normal processor. Slurm: the

10
00:00:38,152 --> 00:00:42,593
scheduler. You ask for resources, and it decides when you run. A partition: a named

11
00:00:42,613 --> 00:00:46,757
group of machines with its own rules. A PI: the principal investigator, the faculty

12
00:00:46,777 --> 00:00:51,218
member who leads a project. And Open OnDemand: a website that gives you the cluster

13
00:00:51,238 --> 00:00:52,110
in a browser.

14
00:00:52,180 --> 00:00:57,785
Start with what it is. AICR stands for AI Compute Resource. Six universities at the

15
00:00:57,805 --> 00:01:01,160
Massachusetts Green High Performance Computing Center, together with the

16
00:01:01,180 --> 00:01:04,910
Massachusetts AI Hub, built one cluster, and they share it.

17
00:01:04,980 --> 00:01:11,003
Inside are two kinds of GPU. Two hundred forty-eight NVIDIA B200s, and one hundred

18
00:01:11,023 --> 00:01:15,320
fifty-two RTX PRO 6000s. That's four hundred GPUs in all.

19
00:01:15,390 --> 00:01:20,635
Around the GPUs: fast storage, a modern software stack for machine learning, and a

20
00:01:20,655 --> 00:01:25,900
CPU-only section for the work that needs no GPU, like cleaning and analysing data.

21
00:01:25,970 --> 00:01:29,885
Northeastern already has its own cluster, called Explorer. Treat AICR as an

22
00:01:29,905 --> 00:01:35,132
extension of it. You develop and test on Explorer. When the code works, you bring it

23
00:01:35,152 --> 00:01:40,050
to AICR for the production run. AICR is shared by every partner university, so it

24
00:01:40,070 --> 00:01:41,690
isn't the place to debug.

25
00:01:41,760 --> 00:01:46,607
Getting on starts with a faculty member. The principal investigator submits a brief

26
00:01:46,627 --> 00:01:50,350
proposal through a request form. Access comes through that project.

27
00:01:50,420 --> 00:01:56,186
Once you're on a project, the easiest door is Open OnDemand, at o o d dot A I C R

28
00:01:56,206 --> 00:02:00,815
dot A I. It sends you to a sign-in page. You choose your identity provider, then

29
00:02:00,835 --> 00:02:03,130
sign in with your Northeastern username and password.

30
00:02:03,200 --> 00:02:07,443
One thing changes: your username. On the cluster it's slightly different, so

31
00:02:07,463 --> 00:02:12,417
accounts from the partner schools stay distinct. If you're j dot smith on Explorer,

32
00:02:12,437 --> 00:02:15,970
you're j underscore smith underscore n e u on AICR.

33
00:02:16,040 --> 00:02:21,345
Your files live in three places, the same three as on Explorer. Home, for small

34
00:02:21,365 --> 00:02:25,605
scripts and your environment. Scratch, for temporary output from running jobs. And

35
00:02:25,625 --> 00:02:28,090
the project directory, for shared, longer-term work.

36
00:02:28,160 --> 00:02:33,304
Home holds a hundred gigabytes. Delete a file there by accident, and you have seven

37
00:02:33,324 --> 00:02:37,780
days to recover it. The project directory has the same seven-day safety net.

38
00:02:37,850 --> 00:02:42,382
Scratch is huge. Ten terabytes, and up to fifteen million files. But nothing in

39
00:02:42,402 --> 00:02:46,609
scratch can be recovered, and there's a thirty-day purge. So move anything important

40
00:02:46,629 --> 00:02:47,910
out before it's gone.

41
00:02:47,980 --> 00:02:53,433
Compute is split into partitions. One is CPU only. Four have GPUs: a batch and a

42
00:02:53,453 --> 00:02:58,907
devel partition for the B200s, and a batch and a devel partition for the RTX cards.

43
00:02:58,927 --> 00:03:02,670
Every GPU node carries about two point three terabytes of memory.

44
00:03:02,740 --> 00:03:07,453
Batch partitions are for jobs you submit and walk away from. They run up to

45
00:03:07,473 --> 00:03:11,554
twenty-four hours, and they support more than one GPU. Devel partitions are for

46
00:03:11,574 --> 00:03:15,340
interactive work: four hours at most, and four running jobs per user.

47
00:03:15,410 --> 00:03:20,697
Before you ask for more than one GPU, confirm your code can actually use them. If it

48
00:03:20,717 --> 00:03:22,570
can't, the extra cards sit idle.

49
00:03:22,640 --> 00:03:27,047
And idle is watched. A background process tracks GPU utilization and cancels jobs

50
00:03:27,067 --> 00:03:31,814
that sit idle. So end your interactive session when you're done. A tool called

51
00:03:31,834 --> 00:03:35,900
jobstats will also report how each job used what it asked for.

52
00:03:35,970 --> 00:03:40,454
You can see the live list yourself. The command s info prints every partition, its

53
00:03:40,474 --> 00:03:45,558
time limit, and its node count. Run it and you may see more partitions than the five

54
00:03:45,578 --> 00:03:47,360
here, because the cluster keeps changing.

55
00:03:47,430 --> 00:03:52,992
AICR runs Slurm, so there are three ways to work. s batch, for jobs that run without

56
00:03:53,012 --> 00:03:57,260
you. s run, for an interactive shell. And Open OnDemand, in the browser.

57
00:03:57,330 --> 00:04:02,790
A batch job is a script. The lines that start with S BATCH are your request: which

58
00:04:02,810 --> 00:04:07,625
partition, how many CPUs, one GPU, sixteen gigabytes of memory, and a time limit of

59
00:04:07,645 --> 00:04:08,270
thirty minutes.

60
00:04:08,340 --> 00:04:13,354
Below the request is the work. Clear the loaded modules. Load CUDA and miniforge.

61
00:04:13,374 --> 00:04:16,230
Activate your environment. Then run your training script.

62
00:04:16,300 --> 00:04:21,339
Submit it with s batch and the file name. If your script writes logs into a folder,

63
00:04:21,359 --> 00:04:25,506
create that folder first. Then three commands follow the job around. s queue shows

64
00:04:25,526 --> 00:04:30,267
your queued and running jobs. s cancel stops one. And s acct shows what a finished

65
00:04:30,287 --> 00:04:31,160
job actually used.

66
00:04:31,230 --> 00:04:36,536
For an interactive shell on a GPU node, use s run. This one asks the RTX devel

67
00:04:36,556 --> 00:04:40,921
partition for eight CPUs, sixteen gigabytes, and one GPU, for one hour. Always set

68
00:04:40,941 --> 00:04:46,247
the time, so the session ends even if you forget. And type exit to give the GPU

69
00:04:46,267 --> 00:04:46,560
back.

70
00:04:46,630 --> 00:04:50,717
Or skip the terminal. Open OnDemand launches graphical sessions, such as JupyterLab

71
00:04:50,737 --> 00:04:55,851
or a desktop. You choose the partition, the time, and the resources, and it submits

72
00:04:55,871 --> 00:05:00,300
the job to Slurm for you. Those sessions count against the devel limits.

73
00:05:00,370 --> 00:05:04,757
To move large data, the primary method is Globus. Northeastern has a subscription.

74
00:05:04,777 --> 00:05:09,163
You set up a Globus account with your Northeastern credentials, then connect a

75
00:05:09,183 --> 00:05:13,570
folder on Explorer, or on your own machine, to a folder on AICR.

76
00:05:13,640 --> 00:05:18,150
Last, the part people forget. Accounts and project directories on AICR are

77
00:05:18,170 --> 00:05:23,435
temporary. Once a year there's a check-in. The PI renews the request, and the

78
00:05:23,455 --> 00:05:24,190
project continues.

79
00:05:24,260 --> 00:05:29,149
And when the project ends, the data has to leave. AICR is not permanent storage.

80
00:05:29,169 --> 00:05:33,403
Move your results back to a Northeastern resource. Questions go to the Research

81
00:05:33,423 --> 00:05:37,330
Computing team, at r c help at northeastern dot e d u.

82
00:05:37,400 --> 00:05:42,195
Let's recap with Claude. AICR is a shared GPU cluster, with two hundred forty-eight

83
00:05:42,215 --> 00:05:47,009
B200s and one hundred fifty-two RTX PRO 6000s. You develop on Explorer, and bring

84
00:05:47,029 --> 00:05:52,512
the production run to AICR. A PI's proposal gets you on, and you sign in through

85
00:05:52,532 --> 00:05:56,983
Open OnDemand. Home holds a hundred gigabytes. Scratch holds ten terabytes, with a

86
00:05:57,003 --> 00:06:00,766
thirty-day purge. Batch partitions run twenty-four hours, devel partitions run four,

87
00:06:00,786 --> 00:06:05,580
and idle GPU jobs get cancelled. Projects are reviewed every year, and your data

88
00:06:05,600 --> 00:06:07,300
goes home at the end.

89
00:06:07,370 --> 00:06:11,702
Your turn. Paste this into Claude: I'm about to run my first production job on a

90
00:06:11,722 --> 00:06:15,783
shared GPU cluster that uses Slurm. Here is my training setup: [describe it in two

91
00:06:15,803 --> 00:06:19,863
sentences]. Write an sbatch script that asks for one GPU and a 30-minute time limit.

92
00:06:19,883 --> 00:06:24,487
Then tell me which of my files belong in home, which in scratch, and which in the

93
00:06:24,507 --> 00:06:28,296
project directory. Then check two things yourself. Does every S BATCH line ask only

94
00:06:28,316 --> 00:06:32,648
for what your code can use? And is anything you can't afford to lose sitting in

95
00:06:32,668 --> 00:06:32,920
scratch?

96
00:06:32,990 --> 00:06:37,250
AICR Introduction. At Nik Bear Brown.
